# Writing for a connected home

Use this guide to write dashboard labels, states, errors, notifications and help for Home Assistant. It follows the GOV.UK writing and style manual, adapted for a household.

Every rule has the same shape: the rule, why it matters, then Before and After examples. The names, rooms and devices in the examples are made up.

## Where to start

| If you are... | Go to |
| --- | --- |
| Deciding what to write | [Writing guidelines](writing-guidelines/index.html) |
| Writing about a state, a request or an error | [States, requests and errors](writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html) |
| Setting out text, images, numbers or files | [Formatting content](formatting-content/index.html) |
| Putting content live, changing it or removing it | [Publish, update or retire content](publish-update-retire/index.html) |
| Checking a word, a number or a Home Assistant term | [Style guides](style-guides/index.html) |

## Use it with an AI tool

Give the AI the pages that match the job. Also give it the real state of the device and the integration’s documented behaviour. An AI cannot know whether a lamp is off or just not reporting. Check every generated message against [States, requests and errors](writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html) before you use it.

The rules are also available as [machine-readable data](../reference/writing-guide.json), and a [review checklist](../reference/writing-guide-checklist.json).

## What the guide does not change

It does not change native Home Assistant labels, translations or the names people give their devices. It applies only to content civica writes. See [Meet household needs](writing-guidelines/meet-household-needs.html).

The earlier [Content design introduction](../styles/content.html) is still available.

